{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "How do I download multiple URLs with threads?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Threads are great for I/O-bound work like HTTP:\n\n```python\nimport requests\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef fetch(url):\n    return requests.get(url, timeout=10).status_code\n\nurls = [\"https://example.com\"] * 10\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=5) as executor:\n    statuses = list(executor.map(fetch, urls))\nprint(statuses)\n```"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Get return values from ThreadPoolExecutor using submit."}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "```python\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\n\ndef work(n):\n    return n * n\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=3) as executor:\n    futures = [executor.submit(work, i) for i in range(5)]\n    for future in as_completed(futures):\n        print(future.result())\n```"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "How do I pass data between threads safely?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Use `queue.Queue`, which is thread-safe:\n\n```python\nimport threading\nimport queue\n\nq = queue.Queue()\n\ndef producer():\n    for i in range(5):\n        q.put(i)\n    q.put(None)\n\ndef consumer():\n    while True:\n        item = q.get()\n        if item is None:\n            break\n        print(\"got\", item)\n\nt1 = threading.Thread(target=producer)\nt2 = threading.Thread(target=consumer)\nt1.start(); t2.start()\nt1.join(); t2.join()\n```"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Create a daemon thread."}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "A daemon thread is killed automatically when the main program exits:\n\n```python\nimport threading\nimport time\n\ndef background():\n    while True:\n        time.sleep(1)\n        print(\"heartbeat\")\n\nt = threading.Thread(target=background, daemon=True)\nt.start()\ntime.sleep(3)  # daemon dies when main exits\n```"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "How do I run code in a separate process?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "```python\nimport multiprocessing as mp\n\ndef worker(n):\n    print(f\"processing {n}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    p = mp.Process(target=worker, args=(5,))\n    p.start()\n    p.join()\n```\n\nGuard with `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` so child processes don't re-run the module."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Use a multiprocessing Pool to parallelize CPU work."}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Multiprocessing sidesteps the GIL, so it's ideal for CPU-bound tasks:\n\n```python\nimport multiprocessing as mp\n\ndef heavy(n):\n    return sum(i * i for i in range(n))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with mp.Pool(processes=4) as pool:\n        results = pool.map(heavy, [100000] * 8)\n    print(results)\n```"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "How do I use ProcessPoolExecutor?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "```python\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\n\ndef cube(n):\n    return n ** 3\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as executor:\n        results = list(executor.map(cube, range(10)))\n    print(results)\n```"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Share data between processes."}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Use `multiprocessing.Queue` or shared values:\n\n```python\nimport multiprocessing as mp\n\ndef worker(q):\n    q.put(\"result from child\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    q = mp.Queue()\n    p = mp.Process(target=worker, args=(q,))\n    p.start()\n    print(q.get())\n    p.join()\n```"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "What is the GIL?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "The GIL (Global Interpreter Lock) is a mutex in CPython that allows only one thread to execute Python bytecode at a time. This means threads can't run Python code truly in parallel on multiple cores. It's fine for I/O-bound work (threads release the GIL while waiting on I/O) but limits CPU-bound work, where multiprocessing is the answer instead."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "When should I use asyncio vs threads?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Use asyncio for high-concurrency I/O-bound work (thousands of network calls) where you control the code and can use async libraries. Use threads for I/O-bound work involving blocking libraries you can't make async (like `requests`). Both are limited by the GIL for CPU work, so for CPU-bound tasks use multiprocessing instead."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "asyncio vs threading vs multiprocessing — which do I pick?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Rough guide:\n\n- asyncio: I/O-bound, very high concurrency, async-aware libraries, single thread cooperative scheduling.\n- threading: I/O-bound with blocking libraries; simpler than asyncio but limited by the GIL for CPU work.\n- multiprocessing: CPU-bound work that needs real parallelism across cores (bypasses the GIL).\n\nMnemonic: I/O-bound -> asyncio or threads; CPU-bound -> multiprocessing."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Why don't threads speed up my CPU-heavy computation?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Because of the GIL: only one thread runs Python bytecode at a time, so CPU-bound code doesn't get parallel speedup from threads. Use `multiprocessing` or `ProcessPoolExecutor` to use multiple cores:\n\n```python\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\n\ndef crunch(n):\n    return sum(i*i for i in range(n))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor() as ex:\n        print(list(ex.map(crunch, [10_000_000] * 4)))\n```"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Is asyncio actually parallel?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "No. asyncio is concurrent but single-threaded; it interleaves tasks on one thread by switching at `await` points. It gives the illusion of parallelism for I/O-bound work but never runs two pieces of Python code at literally the same instant. For true parallelism use multiprocessing."}]}
