{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "What's the difference between concurrency and parallelism?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Concurrency is dealing with many tasks by interleaving their progress (one core, switching between them), while parallelism is actually executing multiple tasks at the same instant on multiple cores. asyncio and threads give concurrency; multiprocessing gives parallelism in Python."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "When is threading better than asyncio?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "Threading is better when you must use blocking libraries that have no async equivalent (e.g. a legacy DB driver or `requests`), since you can run them in threads without rewriting everything. asyncio needs async-aware libraries to actually be non-blocking."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "How do I limit total requests in flight with asyncio and report progress?"}, {"role": "silicat", "content": "```python\nimport asyncio\nimport aiohttp\n\nasync def fetch(session, sem, url, done):\n    async with sem:\n        async with session.get(url) as resp:\n            status = resp.status\n    done[0] += 1\n    print(f\"{done[0]} completed\")\n    return status\n\nasync def main(urls):\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(10)\n    done = [0]\n    async with aiohttp.ClientSession() as session:\n        return await asyncio.gather(*(fetch(session, sem, u, done) for u in urls))\n\nasyncio.run(main([\"https://example.com\"] * 30))\n```"}]}
